ity estimate may be off by orders of magnitude.", "Whether Docta exposes a continuous confidence score or only binary detection is assumed but unverified.", "The ground-truth pattern construction (specific columns, values, z-score levels) may not align with what Docta is optimized to detect.", "Pattern matching thresholds (80%/70% overlap) could materially affect P/R/F1 if Docta's pattern granularity differs from the ground-truth granularity."], "answer": "# Experiment Design: Identifying Rare Patterns in Tabular Data Using Docta\n\n---\n\n## 1. Objective & Scope\n\n**Objective.** Design a protocol-level experiment that evaluates how Docta, used as a black-box pattern-detection tool, identifies rare patterns in a tabular dataset. The experiment must produce quantitative evidence (precision, recall, F1, false-positive rate) and qualitative evidence (human-readable pattern descriptions) sufficient to answer: *what rare patterns does Docta find, how reliably, and what does it add over a trivial baseline?*\n\n**Scope.**\n- Design-only. No executable code, no experiment execution, no claim that any experiment was run.\n- Pseudocode is permitted at the level: *\"Step 3: invoke Docta with parameter set P on preprocessed frame F\u2032, collect output O.\"*\n- Main pipeline covers pattern types A1 (rare value combinations) and A2 (anomalous rows/clusters). Types A3 (association rules) and A4 (temporal sequences) are noted as extensions but excluded from the main pipeline (see \u00a78).\n\n**Out of scope.**\n- Specific Docta internals, algorithm selection, or implementation.\n- Distributed / parallel execution.\n- Real-dataset selection (criteria only, \u00a74.2).\n- A3, A4 pattern types in the main pipeline.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Operationalization of \"Rare Pattern\"\n\nTwo complementary definitions are used. A detected pattern belongs to type A1, type A2, or both.\n\n### 2.1 Type A1 \u2014 Rare Value Combination (RVC)\n\n**Formal definition.**\nGiven a tabular frame F with N rows and column set C = {c\u2081, \u2026, c\u2098}:\n\n- Let S \u2286 C be a subset of columns with |S| = k, where k \u2208 {2, \u2026, k_max} and k_max is a design parameter (default 5).\n- A **value combination** is a tuple v = (v_{c\u2081}, \u2026, v_{c_k}) where v_{c\u1d62} is the value of column c\u1d62 in S.\n- The **support** of v is sup(v) = |{row r \u2208 F : \u2200 c\u1d62 \u2208 S, F[r, c\u1d62] = v_{c\u1d62}}|.\n- The **frequency** of v is freq(v) = sup(v) / N.\n\nA value combination v is a **rare pattern of type A1** if and only if:\n\n> min_support \u2264 sup(v) < \u03c4 \u00b7 N\n\nwhere:\n- **\u03c4** (rarity threshold): default 0.01 (1%). Sweep values in C4: {0.001, 0.005, 0.01, 0.05, 0.10}.\n- **min_support**: default 3. Prevents flagging singletons or near-singletons as \"patterns.\"\n\nThe set of all A1 rare patterns is P_A1 = { v : min_support \u2264 sup(v) < \u03c4\u00b7N, for some S \u2286 C, 2 \u2264 |S| \u2264 k_max }.\n\n**Parameters:** \u03c4, min_support, k_max, column_subset S (or \"auto\" \u2014 let Docta select S).\n\n### 2.2 Type A2 \u2014 Anomalous Row / Cluster (ARC)\n\n**Formal definition.**\nLet C_num \u2286 C be the set of numeric columns in F.\n\n- **Standardization:** For each c \u2208 C_num, compute z_{r,c} = (F[r,c] \u2212 \u03bc_c) / \u03c3_c.\n- **Anomaly score:** For each row r, compute a scalar anomaly score s(r) \u2208 [0, 1] using the method specified in the Docta contract (\u00a73). The method is a Docta-internal choice; the design only requires that s(r) = 0 means \"fully typical\" and s(r) \u2192 1 means \"extreme outlier.\"\n- A row r is an **anomalous row** if s(r) > \u03b8, where **\u03b8** (anomaly threshold) is a design parameter, default 0.95 (95th percentile of the score distribution).\n\nA **anomalous cluster** is a subset R \u2286 {r : s(r) > \u03b8} such that:\n- |R| \u2265 min_cluster_size (default 3), and\n- for all r\u2081, r\u2082 \u2208 R, d(r\u2081, r\u2082) < d_max in the standardized numeric space, where d_max (cluster radius) defaults to 2.0.\n\nThe set of all A2 rare patterns is P_A2 = { anomalous rows where s(r) > \u03b8 } \u222a { anomalous clusters satisfying the above }.\n\n**Parameters:** \u03b8, d_max, min_cluster_size, C_num (numeric column subset, or \"auto\").\n\n### 2.3 Combined Pattern Set\n\nThe full rare-pattern set is **P = P_A1 \u222a P_A2**. A single row or column subset may generate both an A1 and an A2 pattern; these are reported separately.\n\n### 2.4 Extensions (not in main pipeline)\n\n- **A3 (association rules, low support):** Would require a designated target column or itemset semantics. Excluded because the task does not specify a target variable.\n- **A4 (temporal / sequential patterns):** Would require a time-indexed column and a sequence-matching formalism. Excluded because the task does not specify temporal structure.\n\nBoth are noted here for completeness. Adding either requires a data-spec extension and a new ground-truth construction protocol.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Docta Interface Contract & Pipeline\n\nSince no Docta documentation, source code, or installed package exists in the workspace (\u00a78, AS1/AS2), Docta is specified here as a **black box with a documented I/O contract**. The design does not assume or invent any internal method. If the real Docta API differs, this contract must be revised before execution.\n\n### 3.1 Input\n\n| Field | Type | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `frame` | 2D table, N rows \u00d7 M cols | The preprocessed tabular frame F\u2032 |\n| `column_types` | dict: col_name \u2192 {numeric, categorical, boolean, datetime} | Schema metadata |\n| `column_names` | list[str] | Ordered column identifiers |\n| `missing_indicator` | value (e.g., NaN, null) | How missing values are encoded |\n\n### 3.2 Configurable Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Applies to | Description |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `rarity_threshold` (\u03c4) | float | 0.01 | A1 | Maximum frequency for a pattern to be \"rare\" |\n| `min_support` | int | 3 | A1 | Minimum absolute row count |\n| `max_combination_size` (k_max) | int | 5 | A1 | Max columns in a combination |\n| `column_subset` | list[str] \\| \"auto\" | \"auto\" | A1, A2 | Which columns to consider |\n| `anomaly_threshold` (\u03b8) | float | 0.95 | A2 | Score cutoff for anomalous rows |\n| `cluster_radius` (d_max) | float | 2.0 | A2 | Max pairwise distance in cluster |\n| `min_cluster_size` | int | 3 | A2 | Minimum rows in a cluster |\n| `pattern_types` | list | [\"A1\", \"A2\"] | both | Which types to detect |\n\n### 3.3 Output\n\nDocta returns a structured result object O with:\n\n```\nO = {\n  \"patterns\": [\n    {\n      \"pattern_id\":   str,          // unique ID\n      \"type\":         \"A1\" | \"A2\",\n      \"description\":  str,          // human-readable\n      \"involved_columns\": list[str],\n      \"values\":       dict | list[int],  // A1: {col: value}; A2: row indices\n      \"support\":      int,          // number of matching rows\n      \"frequency\":    float,        // support / N\n      \"confidence\":   float,        // Docta internal score, [0, 1]\n      \"anomaly_score\": float | null // A2 only\n    },\n    ...\n  ],\n  \"metadata\": {\n    \"parameters_used\": dict,\n    \"n_rows\": int,\n    \"n_cols\": int,\n    \"runtime_seconds\": float,\n    \"docta_version\": str\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### 3.4 Pipeline (Pseudocode)\n\n```\nStep 1: Load raw frame F. Validate schema (column names, types, N, M).\nStep 2: Preprocess F \u2192 F\u2032:\n         a. Impute missing values (numeric: median; categorical: mode).\n         b. Encode categorical columns (ordinal or one-hot, \u2264 10 categories \u2192 ordinal; > 10 \u2192 one-hot with cap).\n         c. Standardize numeric columns (z-score).\n         d. Record preprocessing transforms for inverse mapping in reporting.\nStep 3: Invoke Docta(F\u2032, params) \u2192 O.\n         params = {\u03c4, min_support, k_max, column_subset, \u03b8, d_max, min_cluster_size, pattern_types}\nStep 4: Post-process O:\n         a. Deduplicate: merge patterns with identical (type, involved_columns, values).\n         b. Filter: remove patterns with support < min_support (safety net).\n         c. Rank: sort by confidence descending.\nStep 5: Emit structured results (tables for \u00a76 metrics) + qualitative top-10 report.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Data Specification\n\n### 4.1 Primary: Synthetic Dataset (with explicit ground truth)\n\n**Dimensions:**\n- N = 100,000 rows (main scenario). Sensitivity: 10,000 and 1,000,000.\n- M = 30 columns:\n  - 15 numeric (c1\u2026c15): drawn from N(\u03bc_i, \u03c3_i\u00b2), with \u03bc_i \u2208 {0, 5, 10, 50} and \u03c3_i \u2208 {1, 2, 5, 10} varied per column.\n  - 10 categorical (c16\u2026c25): 3 to 10 categories each, non-uniform category probabilities.\n  - 5 boolean (c26\u2026c30): P(true) \u2208 {0.1, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9}.\n\n**Missingness:** 5% MCAR (missing completely at random) across all columns.\n\n**Ground truth construction \u2014 embed k = 10 rare patterns:**\n\n5 patterns of type A1:\n| ID | Columns S | Values v | Target support | Target freq |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| GT-A1-1 | {c3, c7, c16} | (42, 7, \"category_X\") | 50 rows | 0.05% |\n| GT-A1-2 | {c5, c12, c20, c26} | (100, 3, \"cat_B\", true) | 150 rows | 0.15% |\n| GT-A1-3 | {c8, c19} | (0, \"cat_Z\") | 400 rows | 0.4% |\n| GT-A1-4 | {c2, c14, c22, c25, c29} | (55, 1, \"cat_D\", \"cat_Q\", false) | 25 rows | 0.025% |\n| GT-A1-5 | {c6, c11, c27} | (10, 50, true) | 800 rows | 0.8% |\n\nConstruction method: After generating the base random frame, **overwrite** the specified rows with the target values. For GT-A1-1, select 50 random rows and set c3=42, c7=7, c16=\"category_X\". Ensure these combinations do not naturally occur in the base data (verify post-generation; regenerate if collision > 5 rows).\n\n5 patterns of type A2:\n| ID | Numeric columns | Anomaly profile | Target size |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| GT-A2-1 | {c1, c5, c10} | z-scores \u2248 +4 (all three) | 30 rows |\n| GT-A2-2 | {c3, c12} | z-scores \u2248 \u22125 (both) | 50 rows |\n| GT-A2-3 | {c7, c14, c15} | z-scores \u2248 +6, mixed sign | 20 rows |\n| GT-A2-4 | {c2, c8, c11, c13} | z-scores \u2248 +3.5 (all four) | 100 rows |\n| GT-A2-5 | {c4, c9} | z-scores \u2248 \u22124 (both) | 15 rows |\n\nConstruction method: Select the target number of random rows, set their numeric column values to \u03bc + (target_z \u00d7 \u03c3). These rows will have high anomaly scores under any reasonable z-score-based or isolation-based method.\n\n**Ground truth file format:**\n```\npattern_id, type, columns, values_or_row_indices, expected_support\nGT-A1-1, A1, \"c3;c7;c16\", \"42;7;category_X\", 50\nGT-A2-1, A2, \"c1;c5;c10\", \"row_indices: [1234, 5678, ...]\", 30\n...\n```\n\n**Reproducibility:** All random draws use a fixed seed (e.g., seed=42). The generation script is specified at pseudocode level with explicit seed, distribution parameters, and overwrite step.\n\n### 4.2 Secondary: Real Dataset (Selection Criteria Only)\n\nNo specific dataset is designated. If a real dataset is later provided, it must satisfy:\n\n| Criterion | Requirement |\n|---|---|\n| Rows | 10,000 \u2013 1,000,000 |\n| Columns | 10 \u2013 100 |\n| Types | Mixed; \u2265 50% numeric or categorical; no free-text columns |\n| Missingness | < 20% per column, < 10% overall |\n| Domain | Any (medical, financial, sensor, operational, etc.) |\n| Validation anchor | Preferably has documented rare events or known anomalies in the literature, enabling partial ground-truth construction |\n\nThe real dataset serves as **secondary validation**: run the same pipeline, report detected patterns qualitatively, and compare against any known rare events. It does not contribute to the primary precision/recall computation.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Controls\n\n### C1 \u2014 Synthetic Ground Truth (Primary Validation)\n\n- **What:** Run the full pipeline (\u00a73) on the synthetic dataset from \u00a74.1.\n- **Comparison:** Match each detected pattern in O against the 10 ground-truth patterns.\n- **Matching criterion:**\n  - For A1: detected pattern matches GT-A1-i if (a) involved columns overlap \u2265 80% with the GT column set, AND (b) values match exactly on the overlapping columns.\n  - For A2: detected pattern matches GT-A2-i if (a) involved numeric columns overlap \u2265 80%, AND (b) row-index overlap \u2265 70% (Jaccard \u2265 0.7).\n- **Output:** TP, FP, FN per pattern and aggregate. \u2192 Precision, Recall, F1.\n\n### C2 \u2014 Uniform Negative Control\n\n- **What:** Generate F_uniform: same dimensions as \u00a74.1 (100k \u00d7 30), but every cell drawn independently from a uniform distribution (numeric: U(0, 1); categorical: uniform over categories; boolean: U(0,1) > 0.5). No embedded patterns.\n- **Run:** Full pipeline on F_uniform.\n- **Metric:** False Positive Rate (FPR) = (number of detected patterns with confidence > 0.5) / (total number of detected patterns). If no patterns detected, FPR = 0.\n- **Expected:** FPR < 0.05. A high FPR here indicates Docta is over-fitting noise.\n\n### C3 \u2014 Ablation: Baseline Without Docta\n\n- **What:** Same synthetic data, same preprocessing. Replace the Docta step (Step 3) with a naive baseline:\n  - **A1 baseline:** Enumerate all column subsets S with |S| \u2208 {2, \u2026, k_max}. For each S, count the frequency of every value combination. Flag combinations with min_support \u2264 sup < \u03c4\u00b7N. (Brute-force; feasible for 30 cols with k_max=5 on 100k rows.)\n  - **A2 baseline:** Compute per-column z-scores. Flag rows where any |z| > 3. Apply DBSCAN (eps=0.5, min_samples=3) on flagged rows in the standardized numeric space to form clusters.\n- **Comparison:** Set difference between Docta patterns and baseline patterns.\n- **Metrics:** \u0394Precision, \u0394Recall, \u0394F1 (Docta \u2212 baseline). Jaccard similarity of pattern sets.\n- **Purpose:** Quantify what Docta adds beyond a trivial frequency-count + z-score approach.\n\n### C4 \u2014 Threshold Sweep (\u03c4 Sensitivity)\n\n- **What:** Run Docta on the synthetic dataset with \u03c4 \u2208 {0.001, 0.005, 0.01, 0.05, 0.10}. All other parameters at defaults.\n- **Track at each \u03c4:** number of detected patterns, precision, recall, F1 (vs. ground truth).\n- **Deliverable:** Table 4 (see \u00a76). Expected trend: precision decreases, recall increases as \u03c4 increases. Identifies the \u03c4 that maximizes F1 for the target application.\n\n### C5 \u2014 Permutation Control (Column-wise Shuffle)\n\n- **What:** Take the synthetic dataset F from \u00a74.1 (with embedded ground-truth patterns). For each column c independently, apply a random permutation to its values across rows: F_perm[r, c] = F[\u03c3_c(r), c], where \u03c3_c is a random permutation of {1, \u2026, N}. This destroys all inter-column co-occurrence structure while preserving each column's marginal distribution exactly.\n- **Run:** Full pipeline on F_perm.\n- **Metric:** FPR = (detected patterns with confidence > 0.5) / (total detected patterns).\n- **Comparison with C2:** C5 is stronger than C2 because the uniform random data (C2) is trivially distinguishable from structured data (different variance, no natural cluster structure). The permuted data (C5) retains realistic marginal distributions, so a pattern detector that merely exploits marginal skew (rather than true co-occurrence) will fail C5 but may pass C2.\n- **Expected:** FPR_C5 \u2265 FPR_C2. If FPR_C5 is significantly higher, Docta may be sensitive to marginal distribution shape rather than true joint structure.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Analysis Plan & Metrics\n\n### 6.1 Primary Metrics (computed on C1)\n\n| Metric | Formula | Computed for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Precision | TP / (TP + FP) | A1 patterns, A2 patterns, aggregate |\n| Recall | TP / (TP + FN) | A1 patterns, A2 patterns, aggregate |\n| F1 | 2PR / (P + R) | A1 patterns, A2 patterns, aggregate |\n\nTP/FP/FN are determined by the matching criterion in \u00a75 C1.\n\n### 6.2 Secondary Metrics\n\n| Metric | Source | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| FPR_uniform | C2 | Fraction of Docta output on uniform data that is false positive |\n| FPR_permutation | C5 | Fraction of Docta output on permuted data that is false positive |\n| \u0394Precision | C3 | Precision_Docta \u2212 Precision_baseline |\n| \u0394Recall | C3 | Recall_Docta \u2212 Recall_baseline |\n| \u0394F1 | C3 | F1_Docta \u2212 F1_baseline |\n| Jaccard(Docta, Baseline) | C3 | |P_Docta \u2229 P_baseline| / |P_Docta \u222a P_baseline| |\n\n### 6.3 Qualitative Report\n\n- **Top-10 patterns** by confidence score from the C1 run.\n- For each pattern, a human-readable description:\n  - Type (A1/A2)\n  - Involved columns (names, not indices)\n  - Specific values or row range\n  - Support count and frequency\n  - Confidence score\n  - One-sentence interpretation: *why this is rare and what it might mean*\n- Flag any pattern that matches a ground-truth entry vs. any that does not (potential novel finding or false positive).\n\n### 6.4 Analysis Procedure\n\n1. Generate synthetic dataset (fixed seed). Verify ground-truth patterns are present (post-generation check).\n2. Run C1: full pipeline on synthetic \u2192 collect O_1.\n3. Run C2: full pipeline on uniform \u2192 collect O_2.\n4. Run C3: baseline pipeline on synthetic \u2192 collect O_3.\n5. Run C4: full pipeline on synthetic with 5 \u03c4 values \u2192 collect O_4a\u2026O_4e.\n6. Run C5: full pipeline on permuted \u2192 collect O_5.\n7. Compute all metrics in \u00a76.1\u20136.2.\n8. Generate Tables 1\u20135 and the Top-10 report.\n9. Write findings narrative.\n\n### 6.5 Output Tables\n\n| Table | Content |\n|---|---|\n| Table 1 | C1: Per-pattern TP/FP/FN, aggregate P/R/F1 (split A1 vs A2) |\n| Table 2 | C2 & C5: FPR, number of spurious patterns, their characteristics |\n| Table 3 | C3: \u0394P, \u0394R, \u0394F1, Jaccard, set-difference patterns |\n| Table 4 | C4: \u03c4, #patterns, P, R, F1 (5 rows) |\n| Table 5 | Top-10 patterns: qualitative descriptions |\n\n---\n\n## 7. Feasibility Assessment\n\n### 7.1 Compute\n\n| Component | 100k \u00d7 30 | 1M \u00d7 30 | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Synthetic data generation | < 1 min | < 5 min | Random draws + overwrite |\n| Preprocessing (impute, encode, z-score) | < 30 s | < 3 min | Vectorized ops |\n| Docta run (per \u03c4) | 1\u201330 min (est.) | 5\u2013150 min (est.) | **Unknown** \u2014 depends on Docta internals |\n| Baseline C3 (brute-force A1 + DBSCAN A2) | < 5 min | < 30 min | A1: C(30,k) for k\u22645 \u2248 174,918 subsets \u00d7 frequency count |\n| C4 sweep (5 \u00d7 Docta) | 5\u2013150 min | 25\u201312.5 h | 5 runs |\n| C2, C5 (2 \u00d7 Docta) | 2\u201360 min | 10\u20135 h | 2 runs |\n| **Total (main scenario, 100k)** | **< 4 hours** | \u2014 | On 224 CPU cores |\n| **Total (sensitivity, 1M)** | \u2014 | **< 1 day** | On 224 CPU cores |\n\nDocta runtime is the dominant uncertainty (AS1). The estimates above assume a polynomial-time algorithm on the table. If Docta uses exponential-time enumeration (e.g., all itemsets), the 1M scenario may be infeasible without pruning.\n\n### 7.2 Memory\n\n- 1M \u00d7 30 cols \u00d7 8 bytes (float64) \u2248 240 MB raw frame.\n- With one-hot encoding (up to ~10 extra columns for high-cardinality categoricals), preprocessing overhead: ~1\u20132 GB.\n- Well within typical server RAM (64+ GB). No memory concern at the specified scale.\n\n### 7.3 Dependencies & Environment\n\n- **Required for execution:** Docta (version TBD), a tabular data library (pandas or equivalent), a numerical library (numpy or equivalent), DBSCAN (for C3 baseline only).\n- **Network is disabled.** If Docta is not pre-installed, an **offline bundle** (wheel/tarball + all transitive dependencies) must be prepared in advance.\n- **GPU is not available and not required.** All operations (frequency counting, z-scores, DBSCAN, Docta) are CPU-suitable at the specified scale.\n- **224 CPU cores** are available. The experiment is embarrassingly parallel across controls (C1\u2013C5 can run concurrently) and across \u03c4 values (C4).\n\n### 7.4 Reproducibility\n\n- Fixed random seed (42) for all synthetic data generation and permutation (C5).\n- All Docta parameters logged in output metadata.\n- Ground-truth file is deterministic given the seed.\n- Pseudocode-level specification is sufficient for reproducibility at the protocol stage; implementation-level reproducibility requires the Docta offline bundle and a pinned environment.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Limitations & Open Questions\n\n### 8.1 Unresolved Assumptions (Blocking for Execution)\n\n| ID | Assumption | Status | Impact if Wrong |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **AS1** | Docta is a real, available tool with a defined API accepting tabular input and returning structured pattern output as specified in \u00a73. | **Unresolved.** No documentation, source, or installed package was found in the workspace. The I/O contract in \u00a73 is a design hypothesis. | If Docta's actual API differs (different parameter names, different output schema, different input format), \u00a73 must be revised. If Docta does not exist or is not publicly available, the experiment cannot be executed. |\n| **AS2** | Docta can process tabular (structured, row-column) data. | **Unresolved.** No evidence in the workspace confirms or denies tabular support. Docta may be designed for text, graphs, images, or other data types. | If Docta is not tabular-capable, the entire design is inapplicable and the question \"identify rare patterns in a tabular dataset using Docta\" has no answer with this tool. |\n\n### 8.2 Design-Level Limitations\n\n- **Protocol-level, not implementation-level.** Without a verified Docta API, this design specifies *what* to do and *what* to expect, not *how* to call the tool. The pseudocode in \u00a73.4 is at the level of \"invoke Docta with parameters P\"; it is not executable.\n- **Ground truth is constructed, not discovered.** The 10 embedded patterns are chosen by the experimenter. They represent a specific notion of \"rare\" (low-frequency combinations, high-z-score outliers). A different construction (e.g., rare patterns in interaction effects, rare patterns in high-dimensional subspaces) would yield different ground truth and potentially different Docta performance.\n- **Pattern matching thresholds are arbitrary.** The 80% column-overlap and 70% row-overlap criteria (\u00a75 C1) are design choices. Sensitivity to these thresholds is not analyzed. A 5% change in overlap threshold could shift a pattern from TP to FP.\n- **Scale ceiling.** The design targets up to 1M rows \u00d7 30 columns. Behavior at 10M+ rows (distributed setting, different algorithmic complexity) is out of scope.\n- **A3/A4 excluded.** Association rules and temporal patterns are not in the main pipeline. If the target application requires these, the design needs a data-spec extension (target variable, time column) and new ground-truth construction.\n- **Single-Docta evaluation.** The design evaluates Docta against one baseline (C3). Comparison against other pattern-detection tools (e.g., dedicated anomaly detection libraries, rule-mining packages) is not included.\n- **No real-dataset validation.** The secondary validation (\u00a74.2) is described by criteria only. Without a specific dataset, generalizability beyond synthetic data is untested.\n\n### 8.3 Open Questions for Future Iteration\n\n1. What is Docta's actual API? (Resolves AS1, AS2.)\n2. Does Docta expose an internal \"rarity\" or \"confidence\" score, or only a binary detect/not-detect? (\u00a73.3 assumes a confidence float.)\n3. Can Docta handle mixed-type columns natively, or does it require pre-encoding? (\u00a73.1 assumes the preprocessor handles encoding.)\n4. What is Docta's computational complexity as a function of N, M, k_max? (Affects feasibility at 1M+ rows.)\n5. Does Docta support incremental / streaming detection, or only batch? (Affects applicability to real-time monitoring.)\n\n---\n\n## 9. Expected Deliverables\n\nUpon execution of this experiment (not performed in this design-only task), the following artifacts are expected:\n\n| # | Deliverable | Format |\n|---|---|---|\n| D1 | **Experiment protocol** (this document, final version) | Markdown / PDF |\n| D2 | **Synthetic data generation specification** (pseudocode, seed, distribution parameters, overwrite step) | Markdown with pseudocode block |\n| D3 | **Ground truth file** (10 patterns, all attributes) | CSV or equivalent table |\n| D4 | **Table 1** \u2014 C1 per-pattern and aggregate P/R/F1 | Table |\n| D5 | **Table 2** \u2014 C2 & C5 FPR and spurious pattern characteristics | Table |\n| D6 | **Table 3** \u2014 C3 ablation: \u0394 metrics, Jaccard, set differences | Table |\n| D7 | **Table 4** \u2014 C4 \u03c4-sweep: #patterns, P, R, F1 per \u03c4 | Table |\n| D8 | **Table 5** \u2014 Top-10 patterns, human-readable | Table + short narrative |\n| D9 | **Findings narrative** (1\u20132 pages: what Docta found, what it missed, what it added over baseline, false-positive behavior) | Markdown / PDF |\n| D10 | **Feasibility memo** (actual compute times, memory, dependency list, offline bundle manifest) | Markdown |\n| D11 | **Updated limitations document** (this \u00a78, annotated with any new findings from execution) | Markdown |\n\n---\n\n*This document is a design artifact. No experiment has been run. No results are reported. All Docta-specific claims are conditional on the I/O contract in \u00a73 being validated against the real tool.*", "evidence": "Workspace inspection confirmed: /workspace contains only task.json (363 bytes). No Docta package (pip list | grep docta \u2192 empty; python3 -c \"import docta\" \u2192 ModuleNotFoundError; find / -name \"*docta*\" \u2192 no results). No data-science packages (pandas, numpy, sklearn absent). Network disabled, GPU disabled, 224 CPU cores, Python 3.14.4. track.md in task.json states \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not require